-- =============================================================================
-- PERMISSION CONFIGURATION - STAGING -> PRODUCTION SYNC (GENERATED FILE)
-- =============================================================================
-- Generated from the STAGING database on 2026-09-16 (database "postgres", role "postgres").
-- The export was READ-ONLY: every read was a SELECT. Nothing was written to staging.
--
-- WHAT THIS CARRIES: permission CONFIGURATION only - access profiles and their
-- capabilities, role defaults, role capabilities, feature eligibility, responsibilities,
-- and the Venture responsibility and scope matrices. Applying it makes staging's
-- finalized permission set become production's default.
--
-- ACTIVE PROFILES ONLY: access_profiles is exported WHERE is_active = 1, and
-- access_profile_capabilities only for those active profiles.
--
-- DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED - DO NOT ADD THEM BELOW:
--   * Per-user tables, NEVER copied: user_capabilities, user_capability_restrictions,
--     user_responsibilities, context_applied_grants, group_memberships, user_groups,
--     contacts, venture_members, venture_staff_assignments.
--     Staging has ~11 contacts, production ~578: copying these would grant or deny
--     THE WRONG PEOPLE.
--   * authz_migrations markers, NEVER copied. Copying staging's ~24 markers would make
--     production's boot-time migrations believe they already ran and silently skip work
--     that never happened.
--
-- DESTRUCTIVE BY DESIGN: every statement is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (<natural key>)
-- DO UPDATE SET ... . This OVERWRITES production's current values for these keys with
-- staging's configuration. That is the intent: staging becomes the default. The file is
-- idempotent - re-running it changes nothing further. Profile references are resolved by
-- NAME at insert time; no raw numeric id from staging appears anywhere in this file.
--
-- KNOWN LIMITATION: responsibilities has UNIQUE(name) AND UNIQUE(key). The conflict target
-- below is (name) only, so if production already holds a DIFFERENT name owning one of these
-- keys, that single statement fails on the unique key and is reported, not silently
-- skipped. Nothing else in this file is affected by that.
--
-- CONTRACT: one statement per line, each terminated by ';', trailing '--' comments only.
-- Apply with: node scripts/db-audit/apply-schema-file.mjs migrations/sync_permission_config_from_staging.sql --apply
-- =============================================================================


